1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,370
The built-in tools cover common ground, but the real power is writing your own.

2
00:00:02,370 --> 00:00:06,000
Let's build a custom JavaScript tool end to end — and see why the description field, not the code, is the real interface.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,643
Go to Automation, Tools, New tool.

4
00:00:07,643 --> 00:00:13,440
A tool is four things: a name, a description, its parameters as a JSON schema, and a JavaScript body that does the work.

5
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:16,000
The app runs that body when the model calls the tool.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,143
Let's write one live — a simple order lookup.

7
00:00:18,143 --> 00:00:21,714
One parameter, an order ID; a few lines of JavaScript that return a status.

8
00:00:21,714 --> 00:00:26,667
Save it, switch it on in a chat, and ask about an order — the model calls your tool and uses the result.

9
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:28,000
You just extended the agent.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:28,714
Now the key insight.

11
00:00:28,714 --> 00:00:29,857
The model never reads your code.

12
00:00:29,857 --> 00:00:33,929
It chooses whether and how to call the tool purely from the name, the description, and the parameter descriptions.

13
00:00:33,929 --> 00:00:36,321
So a vague description like “looks up an order” is a bad interface.

14
00:00:36,321 --> 00:00:39,071
A good one says what it does and when to use it: “Look up an order by its ID.

15
00:00:39,071 --> 00:00:40,607
Use when the user mentions an order number.

16
00:00:40,607 --> 00:00:42,000
” That sentence is doing the real work.

17
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:42,755
That's JavaScript.

18
00:00:42,755 --> 00:00:48,000
If you'd rather use Python — and its libraries — the next episode is the same idea with the one extra requirement Python has.

